 ## Overview
-Proposal Supervision Loss is a regularization technique used in differentiable volume rendering to align the sampling distribution of a lightweight "proposal" network with that of a high-fidelity "target" network (such as a NeRF MLP). It solves the problem of inefficient ray sampling by ensuring the proposal network concentrates samples where the target network predicts significant radiance, even when their respective bin locations differ. It sets itself apart from standard weight-matching losses by operating on the cumulative mass of histograms, allowing for robust supervision despite misaligned sample points [S1].
+Proposal Supervision Loss is a regularization technique that supervises a proposal network by minimizing excess histogram mass that violates a bound established between histograms of 1D data [S1]. It solves the problem of aligning sampling distributions when proposal and target networks use different bin locations, setting itself apart from standard weight-matching losses by operating on cumulative mass envelopes rather than pointwise comparisons [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method minimizes the "excess histogram mass" that violates a theoretical upper bound established between two 1D histograms: one derived from the proposal network's weights $(\hat{t}, \hat{w})$ and one from the target network's weights $(t, w)$.
+The method minimizes the "excess histogram mass" that violates a theoretical upper bound established between two 1D histograms: one derived from the proposal network's weights $(\hat{t}, \hat{w})$ and one from the target network's weights $(t, w)$ [S1].
 
